//? Title: Adaptive Gradient Methods: What the Accumulators Change
//? Subtitle: Exact first steps, anisotropic bowls and learning-rate sensitivity
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A smooth objective can have very different curvature along different coordinates,
so one global learning rate may either oscillate or make little progress.
Adaptive gradient methods rescale coordinates using past gradients and updates.
We derive this library's AdaGrad, RMSProp, AdaDelta, AMSGrad and Nadam variants,
check their first steps on a scalar quadratic, and compare declared settings on
isotropic and stiff bowls. A stability-boundary example shows why a returned
value or a small objective change is not a stationarity certificate.
)
\toc

> The method

Let \(g_t=\nabla f(x_t)\) be the full gradient at the current point. These are deterministic full-batch
implementations, even though their histories arose in stochastic/online learning.
A diagonal rescaling is not a rotation-invariant curvature solve. On a positive
definite quadratic, gradient descent contracts an eigencomponent by 1-eta*h;
convergence with a constant positive rate requires eta*h below two on every axis.

For coordinate j, this implementation uses

$$
 \begin{aligned}
 G_{tj}=G_{t-1,j}+g_{tj}^2,\quad
 x_{t+1,j}=x_{tj}-\eta g_{tj}/\sqrt{G_{tj}+\epsilon}\quad\text{(AdaGrad)},
 \\V_{tj}=\rho V_{t-1,j}+(1-\rho)g_{tj}^2,\quad
 x_{t+1,j}=x_{tj}-\eta g_{tj}/(\sqrt{V_{tj}}+\epsilon)\quad\text{(RMSProp)}.
 \end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(AdaGrad sums squared gradients and places epsilon inside the square root. RMSProp exponentially averages squared gradients and places epsilon outside its square root.)

G never decreases, so its coordinate multiplier decreases. This does not prove
that every actual update decreases: the current gradient also changes. RMSProp
forgets old gradients and can keep moving or oscillating near the solution under
a fixed rate. Epsilon's position and value matter in exact small-gradient checks.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_adaptive_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_adaptive_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_adaptive_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_adaptive_mechanism.svg build/opt_adaptive_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_adaptive_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_adaptive_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(An adaptive method stores coordinatewise history before choosing its next displacement.)
\alttext(The full gradient enters a per-coordinate accumulator. Its reciprocal scale changes each coordinate step. A rotated valley can still require coupled curvature information.)

AdaDelta also averages squared *updates*, starting both histories at zero:

$$
 \Delta x_{tj}=-\frac{\sqrt{D_{t-1,j}+\epsilon}}{\sqrt{V_{tj}+\epsilon}}g_{tj},
 \qquad D_{tj}=\rho D_{t-1,j}+(1-\rho)(\Delta x_{tj})^2.
$$
\alttext(AdaDelta scales the gradient by the ratio of the previous RMS update to the current RMS gradient, with epsilon in both roots, then updates its squared-step average.)

There is no separate global step-size field, but rho, epsilon, starting scale and
the objective still matter. With constant curvature, epsilon supplies the initial
update scale; removing a learning-rate field does not make tuning irrelevant.

AMSGrad keeps m_t=beta1*m_previous+(1-beta1)*g_t and the analogous squared average
v_t. This code takes the maximum of *uncorrected* second moments, then bias-corrects:

$$
 M_t=\max(M_{t-1},v_t),\quad
 \widehat m_t=m_t/(1-\beta_1^t),\quad
 x_{t+1}=x_t-\eta\widehat m_t/(\sqrt{M_t/(1-\beta_2^t)}+\epsilon).
$$
\alttext(The implementation takes a coordinatewise maximum of uncorrected second moments, divides that maximum by the current bias correction, and normalises the corrected first moment by its root plus epsilon.)

M is nondecreasing, but M/(1-beta2^t) need not be. This bias-corrected variant
must not inherit the original paper's convergence theorem without checking its
assumptions. A successful quadratic run is not a test of the published Adam
counterexample. Nadam here uses fixed beta values rather than Dozat's scheduled
momentum products: replace the corrected first-moment direction by
beta1*m_t/(1-beta1^t)+(1-beta1)*g_t/(1-beta1^t), with Adam's corrected second moment.
All five methods test the gradient they just used after applying their update.
Their supplied callbacks must have finite, matching coordinate dimensions.
Work per step is O(p) beyond the gradient cost, with O(p) persistent local state;
every optimize call starts that state from zero.

> Small worked example

>> Two AdaGrad steps are enough to check the accumulator

Use \(f(x)=x^2\), initial \(x=2\), \(\eta=.5\) and \(\epsilon=.01\). The first gradient is
four, G_1=16, and x_1=2-2/sqrt(16.01). The next gradient is 2*x_1 and its square
is added to the same accumulator; a second optimize call would reset that history.
The block runs one two-step solve and prints both its value points and its
pre-update gradient requests. The latter are an explicit diagnostic replay.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_adaptive_two_steps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective f({2}, {0});
  o::AdaGradOptions     a;
  a.step_size                = .5;
  a.epsilon                  = .01;
  a.max_iterations           = 2;
  a.tolerance                = 0;
  const auto          run    = o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::AdaGrad(a), {2});
  const auto          values = run.trace.values();
  std::vector<double> grid   = dm::seql(-.2, 2.2, 201).values(), loss;
  for (double x : grid)
    loss.push_back(f.evaluate({x}));
  auto curve = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .line(grid, loss, "quadratic", {130, 130, 130})
                   .points(
                       values.numeric_column("x0").values(),
                       values.numeric_column("value").values(),
                       "requested values",
                       {40, 100, 190},
                       4)
                   .title("Two AdaGrad steps on x squared")
                   .x_label("x")
                   .y_label("objective")
                   .x_limits(-.2, 2.2)
                   .y_limits(0, 6)
                   .legend({"quadratic", "requested values"}, {{130, 130, 130}, {40, 100, 190}});
  curve.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/opt_adaptive_two_steps.svg");
  std::print(
      "Value requests\n\n{}\n\nPre-update gradients\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",
      values.to_md(8),
      run.trace.sample(f, "gradient").to_md(8),
      run.summary().to_md(8));
}
```
