> Checking the theory

>> Five algorithms on the same isotropic bowl

Use f=sum_j(x_j-centre_j)^2 with centre (4,-2,1) and start (10,10,10).
Every run has a budget of 5000 updates. Rates are explicitly chosen per method:
AdaGrad .5, RMSProp .03, AMSGrad .03 and Nadam .03; AdaDelta keeps rho=.95 and
epsilon=1e-6. These are declared examples, not a rate-optimised ranking. The table
includes final gradient norms, and the curves retain oscillations rather than
turning every trial into a monotonically improving envelope.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_adaptive_isotropic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective f({2, 2, 2}, {4, -2, 1});
  o::AdaGradOptions     a;
  a.step_size      = .5;
  a.max_iterations = 5000;
  o::RMSPropOptions b;
  b.step_size      = .03;
  b.max_iterations = 5000;
  o::AdaDeltaOptions c;
  c.max_iterations = 5000;
  o::AMSGradOptions d;
  d.step_size      = .03;
  d.max_iterations = 5000;
  o::NadamOptions e;
  e.step_size      = .03;
  e.max_iterations = 5000;
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::AdaGrad(a), {10, 10, 10}));
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::RMSProp(b), {10, 10, 10}));
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::AdaDelta(c), {10, 10, 10}));
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::AMSGrad(d), {10, 10, 10}));
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::Nadam(e), {10, 10, 10}));
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"AdaGrad", "RMSProp", "AdaDelta", "AMSGrad", "Nadam"};
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, labels)
      .title("Isotropic bowl: declared settings")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_adaptive_isotropic.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\n", labels[i], runs[i].summary().to_md(7));
}
```
