\alttext(The five callback traces describe a changed metric on the same minimizer. They retain actual sampled losses, including small fixed-rate oscillations.)

>> An objective-change stopping rule can stop at an oscillation

For f=100*x squared, one plain gradient step multiplies x by 1-200*eta.
At eta=.01, x=one becomes minus one, with exactly the same loss and a gradient
of magnitude 200. GradientDescent's small-objective-change test therefore returns
after this first update. At eta=.011 the factor is -1.2, so repeated steps grow
rather than converge. Smaller declared rates contract. The table makes the
returned gradient norm visible instead of calling every normal return convergence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_adaptive_stability.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective           f({200}, {0});
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  const std::vector<std::string>  labels{"eta .001", "eta .005", "eta .010", "eta .011"};
  for (double rate : {.001, .005, .01, .011}) {
    o::GradientDescentOptions s;
    s.step_size      = rate;
    s.max_iterations = 200;
    s.tolerance      = 1e-10;
    runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::GradientDescent(s), {1}));
  }
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, labels)
      .title("Exact scalar stability boundary")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_adaptive_stability.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\n", labels[i], runs[i].summary().to_md(7));
}
```
