\image(figures/opt_adaptive_stability.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Equal objective values do not imply a stationary point.)
\alttext(Rates below 0.01 contract the scalar quadratic. At 0.01 the solver returns after a sign flip with unchanged loss; 0.011 produces growing oscillations over its finite budget.)

> Conclusions and references

The scalar example checks the accumulator and epsilon convention directly.
The two bowls test specific settings, not general superiority, and the stability
boundary supplies an exact counterexample to equating objective-change stopping
with stationarity. The AMSGrad and Nadam formulas above name the implemented
variants explicitly; no stochastic convergence theorem is inferred from these runs.

- J. Duchi, E. Hazan and Y. Singer (2011), [Adaptive Subgradient Methods for Online Learning and Stochastic Optimization](https://jmlr.org/papers/v12/duchi11a.html), JMLR 12, 2121–2159.
- G. Hinton (2012), [Neural Networks for Machine Learning, lecture 6e](https://www.cs.utoronto.ca/~hinton/coursera_lectures.html), RMSProp.
- M. Zeiler (2012), [ADADELTA: An Adaptive Learning Rate Method](https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.5701).
- S. Reddi, S. Kale and S. Kumar (2018), [On the Convergence of Adam and Beyond](https://openreview.net/forum?id=ryQu7f-RZ).
- T. Dozat (2016), [Incorporating Nesterov Momentum into Adam](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=OM0jvwB8jIp57ZJjtNEZ).

> Reading the numerical evidence

`trace_optimize` calls the configured native optimizer through its stated abstract
interface. It records callback requests without inserting fitting evaluations.
Value calls can be rejected trials; gradient calls can be look-ahead points.
`optimization_comparison` explicitly re-evaluates selected points afterwards on
these pure objectives. Those diagnostic calls are excluded from fitting counts.
Callback indices are neither accepted iterations nor wall time. The plotted gap
uses the declared reference (minimum or stated lower bound) and a display floor of 1e-14; that floor does
not modify any optimizer update. Returned coordinates, checked objective and
appropriate stationarity/feasibility checks carry more information than a return
from a finite iteration budget. Native APIs do not expose a uniform stop-status flag.
The recorder retains exceptions and rejects nonfinite requested values/gradients;
this makes failures visible rather than letting an instrumented run continue on NaN.

